# Policies — API.md §Policies
#
# Named bundles of rule lists assignable to clients, optionally on a schedule
# (CONTEXT.md §Policy). Mutations rewrite [[policies]] in
# /config/fastadhunter.toml *before* the change reaches the engine, so a policy
# created here survives a restart.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THESE ARE INDIVIDUAL EXAMPLES, NOT A SUITE. Do not run top to bottom.
#
# Every request below the first mutates persistent state, and three of them
# RECOMPILE THE RULESET — seconds of CPU on the RB5009, because per-rule policy
# masks are built at compile time. Clean up with the last requests in this file.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@base = {{host}}/api/v1
@auth = Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}

### Every policy, with the assignments attached to each.
###
### `lists: null` means every enabled list, NOT "no lists". `timezone` is the
### one schedules are evaluated in. `active_assignments` counts what is in force
### RIGHT NOW — a closed schedule window is excluded, so this reports a boundary
### having passed without waiting for a query to prove it.
GET {{base}}/policies
{{auth}}

# ─── Creating ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Create one. 201 Created. RECOMPILES: a new policy adds a bit to every
### rule's mask, so the ruleset is rebuilt before it can decide anything.
###
### `lists` names a SUBSET of the configured lists; omit it (or send null) for
### every enabled one. Schedule fields are optional here too — an assignment
### without `days`/`start`/`end` is always in force.
POST {{base}}/policies
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "id": "kids",
  "name": "Kids",
  "lists": ["oisd-basic"],
  "assignments": [
    { "client": "192.168.1.50", "days": "mon-fri", "start": "21:00", "end": "07:00" }
  ]
}

### A duplicate id is 409, naming the policy that already holds it.
POST {{base}}/policies
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "id": "kids", "name": "Kids again" }

### `default` is the implicit policy every unassigned client already gets, and
### is reserved — 422, not 409.
POST {{base}}/policies
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "id": "default", "name": "Mine now" }

### Ids are lowercase letters, digits, '.', '_' or '-', and may not start with
### '.' — 422, naming what is allowed.
POST {{base}}/policies
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "id": "Kids At Home", "name": "Nope" }

# ─── Editing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### PATCH leaves absent fields alone. Renaming touches no mask, so this does
### NOT recompile — it is live in milliseconds.
PATCH {{base}}/policies/kids
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Kids (school nights)" }

### Changing `lists` is the one edit that RECOMPILES.
PATCH {{base}}/policies/kids
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "lists": ["oisd-basic", "1hosts-xtra"] }

### `"lists": null` clears the subset back to every enabled list. Also
### recompiles — it is a `lists` change like any other.
PATCH {{base}}/policies/kids
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "lists": null }

### An unknown id is 404, on PATCH and DELETE alike.
PATCH {{base}}/policies/no-such-policy
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "irrelevant" }

# ─── Assigning clients ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# NO RECOMPILE on any of these: an assignment changes no mask, so it is live in
# milliseconds. That is the whole reason assignments are not part of the policy
# body you PATCH.

### The policy in force for one address RIGHT NOW. A client whose schedule
### window is shut reports whatever else covers it — the subnet assignment, else
### `default`. `assignment` is absent when the cover is a subnet or a name
### rather than this exact address.
GET {{base}}/clients/192.168.1.50/policy
{{auth}}

### Assign an address, on a schedule. `start`/`end` are set together or not at
### all; a window that wraps midnight (21:00→07:00) is normal and expected.
PUT {{base}}/clients/192.168.1.50/policy
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "policy": "kids", "days": "mon-fri", "start": "21:00", "end": "07:00" }

### Assign with no schedule — always in force.
PUT {{base}}/clients/192.168.1.51/policy
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "policy": "kids" }

### ONE ASSIGNMENT PER ADDRESS. This does not add a second one: it replaces the
### assignment above, in whatever policy currently holds it. The same client
### listed under two policies would resolve by config order, which is not
### something an operator asking "put this device on kids" is choosing.
PUT {{base}}/clients/192.168.1.50/policy
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "policy": "default" }

### Assigning to a policy that does not exist is 404 — naming the policy, not
### the client.
PUT {{base}}/clients/192.168.1.50/policy
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "policy": "no-such-policy" }

### A malformed address is 400, distinct from the 404 above: the request itself
### is wrong, rather than naming something that is not there.
PUT {{base}}/clients/not-an-address/policy
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "policy": "kids" }

### Clear the assignment. 204.
DELETE {{base}}/clients/192.168.1.51/policy
{{auth}}

### Clearing an address that has no assignment is 404, not a silent 204 — "there
### was nothing to remove" is worth telling apart from "removed".
DELETE {{base}}/clients/192.168.1.51/policy
{{auth}}

# ─── Cleaning up ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Remove the assignment made above, then the policy itself.
DELETE {{base}}/clients/192.168.1.50/policy
{{auth}}

### 204, and RECOMPILES — every rule's mask loses a bit.
DELETE {{base}}/policies/kids
{{auth}}

# ─── The ceiling ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# At most 15 policies may be defined: the compiled ruleset packs policy
# membership into a u16 per rule, and `default` occupies one of the 16 bits
# (crates/fah-config/src/lib.rs, MAX_POLICIES). Creating a 16th is rejected with
# the limit named — the same validation that runs at boot, so a config edited by
# hand cannot get past it either.
